  "version": 1,
  "note": "The anatomy graph (TAXONOMY.md §3). Region → area → structure, with parents, so an item tags at the precision it honestly has and a query rolls up: tag `glute-med-min` and a search for `hip` still finds it. Multi-parent on purpose — the upper trapezius belongs to the neck, the shoulder girdle and the upper back, and forcing one parent is the mistake a tree makes. Every node carries plain-language aliases because the average user types \"butt\", not \"gluteus medius\". Ids are append-only. Depth follows what can be targeted: a group node holds the specific muscles under it wherever a technique can reach them apart (glutes -> maximus, medius/minimus), and `action` nodes name what the body DOES, listing the structures that produce it in `producedBy` -- so \"hip abductors\" and \"glute medius\" both land inside \"glutes\". SEEDED, NOT FINISHED: this covers what the 376-item catalogue actually references and stops there, per §3's guard — a node earns its existence when something can target it distinctly.",
